# en-US: only the messages whose words differ from the house English (en-GB).
# Everything else falls back to English.

# Second paragraph of the words behind the i: how the colours and bars show the work.
activity-help-2 = A program that is working glows and beats, faster and brighter the harder it works: a cool color is a little work, orange is hard work and red is flat out. Round the edge is one bar for each of the processor's threads, growing and warming as it gets busier, so when they are all long and red the computer is flat out and the brightest bubble is why.

# Third paragraph of the words behind the i: how to look at a program and stop it, and what the two grey bubbles are.
activity-help-3 = Click a bubble, or a program in the list, to see what it is doing in plain figures, and to stop it if it has stopped answering: the stop button, pressed twice, asks it to close, and anything not saved in it may be lost. Two quiet gray bubbles hold the rest: Trindle desktop, which is the compositor that draws the screen, the ring, the tab and the wallpaper, and Linux and other programs, which is Linux itself and the programs that look after the computer.

